      This half matters as much as the blocks below. An egress policy that
      refuses everything is indistinguishable from a broken network, and
      would make every result in this script meaningless.

[1;36m==> 2. An UNDECLARED destination — must be BLOCKED[0m
[2m    ------------------------------------------------------------[0m
      https://example.com/                       BLOCKED  ConnectError: 
      https://pastebin.com/raw/abc123            BLOCKED  ConnectError: 
[2m    ------------------------------------------------------------[0m
[1;32m    2/2 undeclared destinations reported BLOCKED[0m
      The refusal comes from web's OWN sidecar, outbound, before a packet
      leaves the pod. Nothing was sent anywhere, so there is no DNS query and
      no TCP connection for a network monitor to notice — which is the
      argument for enforcing egress at the workload rather than at the edge.

[1;36m==> 3. The cloud metadata endpoint — three independent controls[0m
      169.254.169.254 is where an SSRF goes first. Against IMDSv1 one request
      returns the instance role credentials.
[2m    ------------------------------------------------------------[0m
      http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/   REACHED  status=502  body=
      https://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/  BLOCKED  ConnectError: 
[2m    ------------------------------------------------------------[0m
      Three reasons this fails, and all three are real and independent:
      
        1. Istio REGISTRY_ONLY — not a declared destination.
        2. NetworkPolicy — network/04-allow-https-egress.yaml excludes
           169.254.0.0/16 from its 0.0.0.0/0 allowance, so the packet is
           dropped by the CNI even with the mesh out of the way. Plaintext
           port 80 is not permitted outbound at all, which is why the http://
           variant above is refused for a second, different reason.
        3. The instance — terraform sets IMDSv2 required with
           http_put_response_hop_limit = 1, so a response cannot be forwarded
           out of the pod's network namespace. And no IAM role is attached to
           the instance at all, so there would be nothing to read.
      
      Removing them one at a time is how you find out which ones are actually
      load-bearing. Doing that is a worthwhile exercise and it is NOT what
      this script does — it would leave the cluster in a weakened state that
      a later attack would then silently pass through.

